  "transcript": "# Beyond participation: Routes into sport for girls and women\n\n## How easy is it to get in?\n> Digital routes have opened opportunities, but traditional media remain male-dominated and offer few clear routes for young women.\n\nRachel Taylor: Flo, how accessible is sports media for young women now?\nFlo Lloyd-Hughes: It depends. Digital content creation has created ways in, but legacy media—newspapers and big broadcasters—are still older, male-dominated and offer weak routes in for young women.\nFiona Tomas: I agree. There are more women covering women’s sport, and some dedicated roles exist, but breaking into print or broadcast is harder now because jobs are fewer and AI and social media have changed what journalism looks like.\nNatalie Pirks: When I started two decades ago I was often one of one in a press box. There are more women now, which is positive, but traditional paths—study, local paper, climb up—are shrinking.\n\n\n## Does more interest in women’s sport mean more jobs for women?\n> High-profile events boost roles and visibility, but that doesn't automatically widen career opportunities across the industry.\n\nRachel Taylor: Has the viewing rise in women’s sport changed routes in?\nFiona Tomas: Major events create spikes in coverage and new roles, especially in women’s football. But most weeks the back pages remain dominated by men’s sport.\nFlo Lloyd-Hughes: Since the 2022 Euros we've seen national correspondents for women’s football become common. Still, women often get pigeonholed into women’s sport, and commercial decisions can cut coverage quickly.\nNatalie Pirks: Also, putting only your woman on a women’s story can limit how editors treat it—big stories should attract senior editors regardless of gender.\n\n\n## Networks, mentors and who gets seen\n> Mentoring and male allies matter, but recruitment culture, a lack of role models in senior editorial jobs and weak data hamper progress.\n\nRachel Taylor: How important are mentors and networks, and are there enough?\nNatalie Pirks: They’re vital. Groups like Women in Football are invaluable. Male allies matter, too. But newsroom culture—WhatsApp groups, informal networks—can still exclude women.\nAshley Broadley: We track who enters the industry via awards and membership. Right now our SJA membership and shortlists show women at roughly 10–12% in some areas. Recognition matters for visibility.\nLeon Mann: We lack industry-wide data. BCOMS runs academies and masterclasses to create pathways—25 places north, 25 south—because opportunity, not lack of talent, is the block.\nBarbara Slater: Role models help. When broadcasters invest in women athletes and presenters, that credibility opens doors; but you need deliberate recruitment and support, not luck.\n\n\n## Abuse, self-censorship and safety online\n> Online abuse is a major deterrent; women self-censor, employers struggle to protect staff, and witnesses called for stronger platform regulation.\n\nNadia Whittome: How far does gender bias and online abuse shape women’s careers?\nFlo Lloyd-Hughes: It’s huge. Abuse is gendered and often racialised; it forces women to change how they use social media and consider risks before taking public roles.\nNatalie Pirks: I didn't face as much because I worked in news without an opinion voice for years. But once you give opinions, the pile-ons come. That would put many young people off.\nFiona Tomas: I avoid comment sections because they’re misogynistic; many journalists must build a public brand now, which increases exposure to abuse.\nAshley Broadley: Ultimately the social platforms must be held to account; if someone defames a person in print there are remedies — the online space often feels lawless.\nLeon Mann: BCOMS sees co-ordinated pile-ons and believes regulation—like identity checks or stronger moderation—deserves investigation, balanced with safety concerns.\n\n\n## Work conditions: freelancing, maternity and career length\n> The industry's freelance, insecure structure plus travel and caregiving expectations make careers especially hard for women and parents.\n\nRichard Quigley: Does insecure freelance work and lack of typical workplace protections prevent women entering and staying?\nNatalie Pirks: I was lucky to have supportive employers for maternity. Many today are freelance and would be worried. Freelance work offers flexibility but not protection.\nFlo Lloyd-Hughes: Freelancing hits maternity and sickness hard—if you don't work, you don't get paid. Women are often seen as less mobile because of caregiving, which limits opportunities.\nFiona Tomas: Women find it harder to rebuild careers after having children because the job demands travel and irregular hours. Many move freelance for balance but lose security.\n\n\n## Data, apprenticeships and practical solutions\n> Panels want coordinated industry data, more apprenticeships and funded pathways, plus employer actions to shortlist and protect women.\n\nRichard Quigley: What can Government or employers do to encourage women into non-playing roles? Are apprenticeships useful?\nLeon Mann: We need clear data and a plan. BCOMS’s academy got commercial placements—south cohort 100% into paid work, north 70%—because intentional programmes work. We need to scale that.\nAshley Broadley: SJA found adverts and shortlists often discourage women. We changed judging panels and run an academy; representation on shortlists helps but funding is needed to sustain action.\nBarbara Slater: Apprenticeships and targeted interventions can help. Listed-events protections and proportionate tax or broadcast incentives for women’s sport might also stimulate investment and exposure.\nKim Leadbeater: Are women visible in senior decision-making? Does that affect recruitment?\nBarbara Slater: Yes—women in senior roles matter hugely. Where I led BBC Sport we reached 40% by planning. Collaboration across organisations, and allyship, makes a real difference.\n\n\n## What advice would you give a young woman starting out?\n> Be resilient, build networks and expect it to be tough—but also seek mentors and recognition; industry action can make opportunities fairer.\n\nChair: One last hard question: what single thing would help a young woman now trying for a sports journalism career?\nFlo Lloyd-Hughes: Recognition. Women are often not acknowledged for work; awards, commissioning and peer recognition keep people in the game.\nFiona Tomas: Don’t let your gender hold you back. Ask the tough, relevant questions and be unapologetically yourself—good journalism opens doors.\nNatalie Pirks: It’s not for the meek, but it’s getting better. Expect challenges, build a network, and get comfortable with the fact it will take effort and some tough resilience.\nChair: Thank you all—your evidence described clear problems and practical steps: better data, funded pathways, employer protections and action on online abuse.\n",
